2026-04-05 07:05:01.065 | 24908 (24908) | DEBUG    | Processing tool event with attributes: {'gen_ai.tool.name': 'open_files', 'gen_ai.tool.call.id': 'pyd_ai_ab65bebce9cc4c40905d405bc3eef964', 'tool_arguments': '{"file_paths":["AGENTS.md"]}', 'logfire.msg': 'running tool: open_files', 'logfire.json_schema': '{"type": "object", "properties": {"tool_arguments": {"type": "object"}, "tool_response": {"type": "object"}, "gen_ai.tool.name": {}, "gen_ai.tool.call.id": {}}}', 'logfire.span_type': 'span', 'tool_response': '["Successfully opened AGENTS.md:\\n\\n````markdown\\n   0 # Arkan Project Guide\\n   1 \\n   2 ## Overview\\n   3 \\n   4 Arkan is an AI-powered platform for academic and professional writing. It is built using Next.js 15, TypeScript (strict mode), and Google Genkit, strictly adhering to Clean Architecture (DDD) principles for a clear separation of business logic and infrastructure.\\n   5 \\n   6 ## Subagents: Specialist Delegation\\n   7 \\n   8 Tasks can be delegated to specialized agents located in `.rovodev/subagents/` using the `invoke_subagents` tool. This allows for modular development and specialization.\\n   9 \\n  10 ### Delegation Matrix\\n  11 \\n  12 | Subagent                     | Primary Use Cases                                    |\\n  13 | :--------------------------- | :--------------------------------------------------- |\\n  14 | Next.js 15 AI/Server Actions | App Router, data fetching, `use server` patterns, Genkit flows. |\\n  15 | shadcn/ui Builder            | UI components, Radix primitives, CVA, accessibility.   |\\n  16 | Data Table/Dashboard         | Admin dashboards, TanStack Table, Recharts.          |\\n  17 | Real-time/WebSocket          | SSE, WebSockets, streaming endpoints.                |\\n  18 | State Management             | Zustand stores (`useBuilderStore`), state architecture.   |\\n  19 | Bug Hunter                   | Reproducing bugs, analyzing `errors.txt`, detailed reporting. |\\n  20 | Code Review/Best Practices   | Security audits, refactoring, code quality, consistency. |\\n  21 | Scalability Advisor          | RAG/Qdrant optimization, API bottleneck analysis.    |\\n  22 \\n  23 A common pattern for delegating new features is to split tasks among: **AI Dev** (for core logic), **shadcn/ui** (for component implementation), and **Server Actions** (for form handling).\\n  24 \\n  25 ## Architecture & Logic\\n  26 \\n  27 ### Clean Architecture (DDD)\\n  28 \\n  29 Modules within `src/modules/` adhere to a four-layer structure:\\n  30 \\n  31 1.  **`domain/`**: Contains pure entities, repository interfaces, and Zod schemas. This layer is free from external dependencies.\\n  32 2.  **`application/`**: Houses use cases that orchestrate domain logic.\\n  33 3.  **`adapters/`**: Includes API clients, controllers, and localized Zustand stores, acting as bridges to external services.\\n  34 4.  **`infrastructure/`**: Provides concrete implementations for services like Genkit, SQLite/Drizzle, and Qdrant.\\n  35 \\n  36 ### Dependency Injection (DI)\\n  37 \\n  38 The project utilizes `tsyringe` for dependency injection.\\n  39 \\n  40 *   **Registration**: Services are registered using `@injectable()` and `@inject()` decorators, with all registrations centralized in `src/infrastructure/di/container.ts`.\\n  41 *   **Server-Only Enforcement**: To prevent client-side contamination, server-only modules are loaded using `require()` within a `typeof window === \'undefined\'` check in `container.ts`.\\n  42 *   **Resolution**: Services should not be instantiated directly using `new`. Instead, resolve them using the `useService(token)` hook.\\n  43 \\n  44 ### AI & Genkit Flows\\n  45 \\n  46 *   **Implementation**: All AI-related flows are located in `src/modules/ai/infrastructure/flows/` and are prefixed with `\'use server\'`.\\n  47 *   **Safety & Output**: To ensure safe and predictable AI output, use `Result<T, E>` objects and access their values via `.getValue()`. The `cleanAiOutput` utility function should be used to strip extraneous conversational elements.\\n  48 *   **Consistency**: Adherence to `MODEL_BY_TASK` constants is mandatory for all AI calls to maintain uniformity.\\n  49 \\n  50 ## State Management & Robustness\\n  51 \\n  52 ### Atomic State Updates\\n  53 \\n  54 *   **Batching**: For multi-node updates (e.g., bulk context mapping), operations must be batched within a single `updateProject` callback. This is typically achieved using functional reduction (e.g., `targets.reduce(...)`) to guarantee atomicity.\\n  55 *   **Selection Recovery**: Structural changes, particularly deletions, must trigger corresponding callbacks (e.g., `onSelectChapter(null)`) to clear workspace focus. This prevents orphaned UI states and ensures data integrity.\\n  56 \\n  57 ### Defensive Programming\\n  58 \\n  59 *   **Hook Compliance**: All hooks (including `useMemo`, `useCallback`, and `useAiActions`) must be placed at the top level of a component, *before* any conditional rendering or early returns. This is a strict requirement for React 19 stability.\\n  60 *   **Error Handling & Persistence**: Mutations and external service calls (e.g., export, generation) must be wrapped in `try-catch` blocks. Explicit UI feedback, such as toast notifications, should be provided to the user in case of errors.\\n  61 *   **ID Standardization**: The `@/infrastructure/utils/randomId` utility must be used exclusively for generating entity IDs. Avoid using `uuidv4` or timestamps for this purpose.\\n  62 \\n  63 ## Coding & UI Standards\\n  64 \\n  65 *   **TypeScript**: The project enforces a strict TypeScript policy with zero tolerance for the `any` type. Use `unknown` and implement proper narrowing techniques, including explicit `instanceof` guards for domain entities.\\n  66 *   **Components**: Server Components are the default. For high-frequency workspace components, `React.memo` should be used to optimize performance.\\n  67 *   **Styling**: Tailwind CSS is the standard. The \\"Intelligence Suite\\" aesthetic requires specific styling: 28px dot-grids, high-density labels (`text-[10px]`, `font-black`, `uppercase`), and \\"Stable Manifest\\" footers.\\n  68 *   **API Responses**: All API responses must be standardized by utilizing the `handle*Result` functions located in `src/infrastructure/api/api-utils.ts`.\\n  69 \\n  70 ## RAG & Infrastructure\\n  71 \\n  72 *   **Vector Store**: The project utilizes Qdrant as its vector store, with a collection named `arkan_vectors` and dimensions set to 768.\\n  73 *   **Storage**: Local file storage is managed via `storage/uploads/`. Robust path-traversal protection is a mandatory security requirement for this directory.\\n  74 *   **Internationalization (i18n)**: Support for both Left-to-Right (LTR) and Right-to-Left (RTL) layouts is provided through the `LanguageDirectionManager`.\\n  75 \\n  76 ---\\n  77 \\n  78 ## Change Log\\n  79 \\n  80 *   **Consolidation**: The \\"Robustness & State Patterns\\" section has been integrated into the core \\"Architecture & Logic\\" and \\"State Management & Robustness\\" sections for a more cohesive structure.\\n  81 *   **Deduplication**: Redundant descriptions of folder structures and repeated emphasis on `instanceof` guards and the `randomId` utility have been removed.\\n  82 *   **Structure**: Technical requirements have been logically grouped into distinct sections: \\"Architecture & Logic,\\" \\"State Management & Robustness,\\" \\"Coding & UI Standards,\\" and \\"RAG & Infrastructure.\\"\\n  83 *   **Refinement**: Phrasing related to Hook compliance and ID standardization has been tightened to reflect authoritative development requirements.\\n````"]'}
